<blockquote>
<p>All known SCP-402-A</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I realize that this refers to a singular volume of water, but the use of singular here feels odd to me.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Mobile Task Force Tau-4 &quot;Water, Water, Everywhere&quot; is tasked</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Typically, the MTF nicknames are also written (in parentheses), but I supposed it's just stylistic.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>is tasked with performing all testing</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I usually associate MTFs with field work, as opposed to testing or other lab work.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Any water that comes in contact with SCP-402 (hereby SCP-402-A) develops antimemetic properties, effectively rendering it imperceptible by any direct means.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How does the Foundation know this? How do you perceive the imperceptible?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Safe handling of SCP-402-A can only be performed by trained personnel.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>By &quot;can&quot; do you mean &quot;permitted&quot; or &quot;able to&quot;, because I'd agree with the first, but not necessarily the second.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>resulting in the illusion that it did not possess any body fluids and giving it a desiccated appearance.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why would he look dessicated? Presumably the anomalous water still acts and moves like water, so it's not as if he'd look like a mummy. Also: if the body can't recognize the water and trigger laryngospasm, how are his cells still able to recognize and use it as water?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-402-1's blood, when composed of SCP-402-A, possesses cognitohazardous effects when consumed in any quantity. Subjects will become convinced that any water they encounter is an extension of an effectively infinite subterranean body of water, in which they risk being trapped should they come into contact with the water. This results in severe aquaphobia, as well as a fear of drowning that persists even in situations where no water is present</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This phenomenon seems sort of tacked on and underdeveloped.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Testing the anomalous properties of SCP-402-1's blood has been deemed cost-ineffective</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This reads like a cop-out. For the Foundation to exist in any reasonable capacity it would need a functionally infinite operating budget, and I doubt they'd let money get in the way of further investigating the nature of an anomaly.</p>
<p>Also: While I feel the crosslinks feel a little forced, I don't really have an issue with them both due to the ongoing crosslinking effort, and due to a recognition of deeply ingrained site-culture bias.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
I appreciate the attempt made to rewrite a fairly bare-bones scip in a style reminiscent of some of the older articles, but I feel like it leans more towards dated than classic. No vote for now.
							</div>

								
