<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>(miscellaneous stylistic concerns)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Appreciated, but I think those are all fine.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>How does the Foundation know this? How do you perceive the imperceptible?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Indirect means, which the paragraph then goes on to list examples of.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>By &quot;can&quot; do you mean &quot;permitted&quot; or &quot;able to&quot;, because I'd agree with the first, but not necessarily the second.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I do mean the second. People who can't exclude as much moisture as possible from the surrounding area, keep a close eye on <em>indirect</em> signs of where water might be, and recognize the effects of oxygen deprivation quickly all risk a 402-A-related mishap.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Why would he look dessicated? Presumably the anomalous water still acts and moves like water, so it's not as if he'd look like a mummy.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I could BS something about refractive indices and how much of cells are water or whatever, but quite honestly I just want it to be this way and it's within the realm of possibility, so I took the creepier option.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Also: if the body can't recognize the water and trigger laryngospasm, how are his cells still able to recognize and use it as water?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Laryngospasm is a response to a tactile sensation, whereas the body's cells react to the chemical properties of water.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This phenomenon seems sort of tacked on and underdeveloped.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In conjunction with the 109 and 812 crosslinks (which already linked up with 402, incidentally), it's worldbuilding. Something fishy is going on with a possibly-imaginary underground sea that may or may not be leaking into the rest of the world. Delving too deeply into this would be too distracting from the original premise, IMO &#8212; overdevelopment, basically.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This reads like a cop-out. For the Foundation to exist in any reasonable capacity it would need a functionally infinite operating budget, and I doubt they'd let money get in the way of further investigating the nature of an anomaly.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>High resources overall does not imply high resources earmarked for testing anomalous properties on human subjects when it's totally unnecessary for containment. There are places those researchers can be assigned where they'll be keeping things contained and saving lives. The Foundation doesn't always care about everything.</p>
</div>
